  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2)+8 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
